{"parentUuid":"58eec8fd-48de-45bc-b964-868f7fa08736","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"7d32c620-11f6-4aa7-be6a-85804d3c0347","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"## Today\\nSaturday, October 03, 2026\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Knowledge Base\\n\\n319 compiled articles (237 concepts, 81 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here \\u2014 read it only when a question actually needs it:\\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \\\"<query>\\\" --top-k 5`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Recent Daily Log\\n\\n- Optional: confirm `SoftwareTech_Review` should stay `institutional` rather than `personal`.\\n\\n### Memory Flush (09:53)\\n\\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\\n\\n### Session (09:53)\\n\\n**Context:** Closed out a Mac session: ran the memory compiler, ran `/dev-sync`, and checked open hook failures. Flint is the active project, waiting on a Windows-only Jev run.\\n\\n**Key Exchanges:**\\n- Asked if the memory compiler needed a Mac run after Windows already ran it. Answer: yes. Each machine keeps its own daily logs and its own articles, and git does not sync them.\\n- Asked if hook failures were still open. Answer: all fixed.\\n\\n**Decisions Made:**\\n- Ran `compile.py` on the Mac. It compiled 3 waiting logs (9/27, 9/29, 9/30) with no failures. Articles went 298 \\u2192 318. Cost $3.68.\\n- Dropped the Mac's duplicate `42Analytics` index line and kept the Windows line instead, to avoid a conflict.\\n- Left `~/.claude/settings.json` uncommitted on purpose, so a Mac-only hook path does not reach Windows.\\n\\n**Lessons Learned:**\\n- The compiler repo syncs through GitHub, but the daily logs and knowledge articles are per-machine. A run on one machine never covers the other.\\n- Something on the Mac re-adds a second `dcg` guard hook with a Mac-only path. It came back within seconds of removal. Likely `dcg` re-installing itself.\\n- `42Analytics` feeds Flint. Windows finished tier A waves 9\\u201311.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- On Windows, in `Desktop\\\\DEV\\\\Flint`, run in order: `uv run scripts/triage_model_pass.py`, `uv run scripts/jev_pass.py state`, `uv run scripts/jev_pass.py run`. The run skips the 284 files Jev already did.\\n- After the Jev run: Eric labels the answers, then Claude approves the shortlist.\\n- Watch tonight's 21:00 `daily-backup` run. It is the first test of the two fixes (`global-memory` behind GitHub, `42Analytics` missing from the backup list).\\n- If the duplicate `dcg` hook returns in `settings.json`, investigate the cause.\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"uv run python hooks/session-start.py","durationMs":178},"type":"attachment","uuid":"af4f0114-fff2-4bf0-a4b7-29338f572aa4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:24:45.772Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"c30dd791-2ba7-4d19-98b5-461f15f5a10e","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"af4f0114-fff2-4bf0-a4b7-29338f572aa4","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"7d32c620-11f6-4aa7-be6a-85804d3c0347","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\\n# User Profile\\n\\n## About\\nEric Rash, Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds football GPS and force-plate pipelines. Wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo on 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-first player-coach near Iowa, $90K+ floor, mid-2027. Read `~/DEV/ContextVault` before career or personal advice.\\n\\n## Preferences\\n- Python/R. Two machines: Windows by day, Mac by evening (split 9/24). Tool paths: machine.md.\\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB. No cloud servers or schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Google Drive only; keep DuckDB local to avoid sync locks.\\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\\n- Staff-facing text: Athletic Performance (not S&C), Athletic Medicine (not sports medicine), Applied Performance (not sport science).\\n\\n## Working Style\\n- Direct; says \\\"stop\\\" when done. Challenge vague or rehearsed reasoning: \\\"sharpened, not soothed.\\\"\\n- Decide standard technical details, state the choice, and move on.\\n- Build in small steps. Do not break mid-season work.\\n- Authors direction when leadership does not; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo group.\\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\\n# Working Memory\\n\\n## Active Threads (max 5; park one to add one)\\n- **Status report**: biweekly to 12/18. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline\\n- **SW/Tech Review (10/1)**: Erin asked; NEXT Teams 10/2, ET by 11/30. →`SoftwareTech_Review/docs/`\\n- **Ship queue (9/25)**: q2wk to a named user: RI5 #002 wk 9/28, intern M1 10/9, AP Digest ch1 10/23. →`RunningTheRoom/docs/STATUS.md`\\n- **PP Recruiting (9/30)**: 017 sent-ready; 018 SHIP. NEXT send 018, walkthrough. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/`\\n- **RTP CLA (9/26)**: L1–2 built. NEXT Tim/Jacob export; Joe → L3. →`RTP_Constraints_Course/docs/`\\n\\n## Parked (NEXT lives in each STATUS.md; `where-was-i.py` lists them)\\n- Prioritization slate LIVE 5/5 →Tier A prioritization-slate\\n- Personal site: scroll-craft LOCAL, Artifact live →Tier A personal-site-build-routes\\n- FB matchups: EPA = PFF via `scripts/pff_epa_profile.py` →`MatchupAnalysis_FB2026`\\n- Flint: NEXT Eric: OneDrive keep/drop, 2 verify →`Flint`\\n- 42Analytics: NEXT captions (429s) on Mac →`42Analytics`\\n- VB GRID: fix merged 10/2 (#2); await Mike's reply →`Volleyball_GRID`\\n- Sleep sched: NEXT night/early-kickoff →`sleep-scheduler`\\n- TypeSafe: MAINTENANCE; PR #31 merged as record →`TypeSafe_Sandbox`\\n\\n## Environment Notes\\n- Machine split (9/24): Win=day, Mac=eve; Mac sole writer GPS/Perch/VALD.\\n- Hooks: patch both copies for shared events. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\\n- daily-backup: manifest entries alone copy nothing; sweep nested ~/.claude repos →handoff 09-19.\\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private I-983 repo; folder has a space, repo an underscore.\\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\\n- Win: Drive sync, junction and renv traps →machine.md. PFF: swap Parquet on Drive, never the DB; `PFF_DB`.\\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture Mac only. Dashboard →command-centre.\\n- Post-merge: delete merged branches unasked (verify) →Tier A post-merge-cleanup.\\n- Sessions: start `where-was-i.py`, prune with `session-sweep.py` →Tier A session-hygiene.\\n\\n## Pending Decisions\\n- None open. (OKR gate + OKRs_2026-27 CLOSED 10/2: Eric not developing further.)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\\n\\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4310 chars, updated 2026-10-02 11:26)\"}}","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading unified cross-harness snapshot...","durationMs":178},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6b6c3dd4-e70c-4ac5-af96-f722c610a1e1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:24:45.772Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"c30dd791-2ba7-4d19-98b5-461f15f5a10e","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"6b6c3dd4-e70c-4ac5-af96-f722c610a1e1","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"7d32c620-11f6-4aa7-be6a-85804d3c0347","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n  <ctx_commands>\\n    \\\"ctx stats\\\" | \\\"ctx-stats\\\" | \\\"/ctx-stats\\\" | context savings question\\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx doctor\\\" | \\\"ctx-doctor\\\" | \\\"/ctx-doctor\\\" | diagnose context-mode\\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx upgrade\\\" | \\\"ctx-upgrade\\\" | \\\"/ctx-upgrade\\\" | update context-mode\\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx purge\\\" | \\\"ctx-purge\\\" | \\\"/ctx-purge\\\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\\n\\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \\\"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\\\"\\n  </ctx_commands>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/sessionstart.mjs\"","durationMs":255},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b934cc8e-241b-46cd-b094-a46923da6f06","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:24:45.849Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"c30dd791-2ba7-4d19-98b5-461f15f5a10e","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"b934cc8e-241b-46cd-b094-a46923da6f06","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["## Today\nSaturday, October 03, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n319 compiled articles (237 concepts, 81 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n- Optional: confirm `SoftwareTech_Review` should stay `institutional` rather than `personal`.\n\n### Memory Flush (09:53)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Session (09:53)\n\n**Context:** Closed out a Mac session: ran the memory compiler, ran `/dev-sync`, and checked open hook failures. Flint is the active project, waiting on a Windows-only Jev run.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Asked if the memory compiler needed a Mac run after Windows already ran it. Answer: yes. Each machine keeps its own daily logs and its own articles, and git does not sync them.\n- Asked if hook failures were still open. Answer: all fixed.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Ran `compile.py` on the Mac. It compiled 3 waiting logs (9/27, 9/29, 9/30) with no failures. Articles went 298 → 318. Cost $3.68.\n- Dropped the Mac's duplicate `42Analytics` index line and kept the Windows line instead, to avoid a conflict.\n- Left `~/.claude/settings.json` uncommitted on purpose, so a Mac-only hook path does not reach Windows.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- The compiler repo syncs through GitHub, but the daily logs and knowledge articles are per-machine. A run on one machine never covers the other.\n- Something on the Mac re-adds a second `dcg` guard hook with a Mac-only path. It came back within seconds of removal. Likely `dcg` re-installing itself.\n- `42Analytics` feeds Flint. Windows finished tier A waves 9–11.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- On Windows, in `Desktop\\DEV\\Flint`, run in order: `uv run scripts/triage_model_pass.py`, `uv run scripts/jev_pass.py state`, `uv run scripts/jev_pass.py run`. The run skips the 284 files Jev already did.\n- After the Jev run: Eric labels the answers, then Claude approves the shortlist.\n- Watch tonight's 21:00 `daily-backup` run. It is the first test of the two fixes (`global-memory` behind GitHub, `42Analytics` missing from the backup list).\n- If the duplicate `dcg` hook returns in `settings.json`, investigate the cause.\n","## Today\nSaturday, October 03, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n319 compiled articles (237 concepts, 81 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n- Optional: confirm `SoftwareTech_Review` should stay `institutional` rather than `personal`.\n\n### Memory Flush (09:53)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Session (09:53)\n\n**Context:** Closed out a Mac session: ran the memory compiler, ran `/dev-sync`, and checked open hook failures. Flint is the active project, waiting on a Windows-only Jev run.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Asked if the memory compiler needed a Mac run after Windows already ran it. Answer: yes. Each machine keeps its own daily logs and its own articles, and git does not sync them.\n- Asked if hook failures were still open. Answer: all fixed.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Ran `compile.py` on the Mac. It compiled 3 waiting logs (9/27, 9/29, 9/30) with no failures. Articles went 298 → 318. Cost $3.68.\n- Dropped the Mac's duplicate `42Analytics` index line and kept the Windows line instead, to avoid a conflict.\n- Left `~/.claude/settings.json` uncommitted on purpose, so a Mac-only hook path does not reach Windows.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- The compiler repo syncs through GitHub, but the daily logs and knowledge articles are per-machine. A run on one machine never covers the other.\n- Something on the Mac re-adds a second `dcg` guard hook with a Mac-only path. It came back within seconds of removal. Likely `dcg` re-installing itself.\n- `42Analytics` feeds Flint. Windows finished tier A waves 9–11.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- On Windows, in `Desktop\\DEV\\Flint`, run in order: `uv run scripts/triage_model_pass.py`, `uv run scripts/jev_pass.py state`, `uv run scripts/jev_pass.py run`. The run skips the 284 files Jev already did.\n- After the Jev run: Eric labels the answers, then Claude approves the shortlist.\n- Watch tonight's 21:00 `daily-backup` run. It is the first test of the two fixes (`global-memory` behind GitHub, `42Analytics` missing from the backup list).\n- If the duplicate `dcg` hook returns in `settings.json`, investigate the cause.\n","## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash, Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds football GPS and force-plate pipelines. Wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo on 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-first player-coach near Iowa, $90K+ floor, mid-2027. Read `~/DEV/ContextVault` before career or personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python/R. Two machines: Windows by day, Mac by evening (split 9/24). Tool paths: machine.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB. No cloud servers or schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Google Drive only; keep DuckDB local to avoid sync locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n- Staff-facing text: Athletic Performance (not S&C), Athletic Medicine (not sports medicine), Applied Performance (not sport science).\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Challenge vague or rehearsed reasoning: \"sharpened, not soothed.\"\n- Decide standard technical details, state the choice, and move on.\n- Build in small steps. Do not break mid-season work.\n- Authors direction when leadership does not; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo group.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads (max 5; park one to add one)\n- **Status report**: biweekly to 12/18. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline\n- **SW/Tech Review (10/1)**: Erin asked; NEXT Teams 10/2, ET by 11/30. →`SoftwareTech_Review/docs/`\n- **Ship queue (9/25)**: q2wk to a named user: RI5 #002 wk 9/28, intern M1 10/9, AP Digest ch1 10/23. →`RunningTheRoom/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **PP Recruiting (9/30)**: 017 sent-ready; 018 SHIP. NEXT send 018, walkthrough. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/`\n- **RTP CLA (9/26)**: L1–2 built. NEXT Tim/Jacob export; Joe → L3. →`RTP_Constraints_Course/docs/`\n\n## Parked (NEXT lives in each STATUS.md; `where-was-i.py` lists them)\n- Prioritization slate LIVE 5/5 →Tier A prioritization-slate\n- Personal site: scroll-craft LOCAL, Artifact live →Tier A personal-site-build-routes\n- FB matchups: EPA = PFF via `scripts/pff_epa_profile.py` →`MatchupAnalysis_FB2026`\n- Flint: NEXT Eric: OneDrive keep/drop, 2 verify →`Flint`\n- 42Analytics: NEXT captions (429s) on Mac →`42Analytics`\n- VB GRID: fix merged 10/2 (#2); await Mike's reply →`Volleyball_GRID`\n- Sleep sched: NEXT night/early-kickoff →`sleep-scheduler`\n- TypeSafe: MAINTENANCE; PR #31 merged as record →`TypeSafe_Sandbox`\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Machine split (9/24): Win=day, Mac=eve; Mac sole writer GPS/Perch/VALD.\n- Hooks: patch both copies for shared events. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- daily-backup: manifest entries alone copy nothing; sweep nested ~/.claude repos →handoff 09-19.\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private I-983 repo; folder has a space, repo an underscore.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Win: Drive sync, junction and renv traps →machine.md. PFF: swap Parquet on Drive, never the DB; `PFF_DB`.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture Mac only. Dashboard →command-centre.\n- Post-merge: delete merged branches unasked (verify) →Tier A post-merge-cleanup.\n- Sessions: start `where-was-i.py`, prune with `session-sweep.py` →Tier A session-hygiene.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- None open. (OKR gate + OKRs_2026-27 CLOSED 10/2: Eric not developing further.)\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4310 chars, updated 2026-10-02 11:26)","\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"cab8040e-b8bb-4ebb-a3e7-1df65f035770","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:24:45.850Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nSessionStart hook additional context: ## Today\nSaturday, October 03, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n319 compiled articles (237 concepts, 81 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n- Optional: confirm `SoftwareTech_Review` should stay `institutional` rather than `personal`.\n\n### Memory Flush (09:53)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Session (09:53)\n\n**Context:** Closed out a Mac session: ran the memory compiler, ran `/dev-sync`, and checked open hook failures. Flint is the active project, waiting on a Windows-only Jev run.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Asked if the memory compiler needed a Mac run after Windows already ran it. Answer: yes. Each machine keeps its own daily logs and its own articles, and git does not sync them.\n- Asked if hook failures were still open. Answer: all fixed.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Ran `compile.py` on the Mac. It compiled 3 waiting logs (9/27, 9/29, 9/30) with no failures. Articles went 298 → 318. Cost $3.68.\n- Dropped the Mac's duplicate `42Analytics` index line and kept the Windows line instead, to avoid a conflict.\n- Left `~/.claude/settings.json` uncommitted on purpose, so a Mac-only hook path does not reach Windows.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- The compiler repo syncs through GitHub, but the daily logs and knowledge articles are per-machine. A run on one machine never covers the other.\n- Something on the Mac re-adds a second `dcg` guard hook with a Mac-only path. It came back within seconds of removal. Likely `dcg` re-installing itself.\n- `42Analytics` feeds Flint. Windows finished tier A waves 9–11.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- On Windows, in `Desktop\\DEV\\Flint`, run in order: `uv run scripts/triage_model_pass.py`, `uv run scripts/jev_pass.py state`, `uv run scripts/jev_pass.py run`. The run skips the 284 files Jev already did.\n- After the Jev run: Eric labels the answers, then Claude approves the shortlist.\n- Watch tonight's 21:00 `daily-backup` run. It is the first test of the two fixes (`global-memory` behind GitHub, `42Analytics` missing from the backup list).\n- If the duplicate `dcg` hook returns in `settings.json`, investigate the cause.\n\n## Today\nSaturday, October 03, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n319 compiled articles (237 concepts, 81 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n- Optional: confirm `SoftwareTech_Review` should stay `institutional` rather than `personal`.\n\n### Memory Flush (09:53)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Session (09:53)\n\n**Context:** Closed out a Mac session: ran the memory compiler, ran `/dev-sync`, and checked open hook failures. Flint is the active project, waiting on a Windows-only Jev run.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Asked if the memory compiler needed a Mac run after Windows already ran it. Answer: yes. Each machine keeps its own daily logs and its own articles, and git does not sync them.\n- Asked if hook failures were still open. Answer: all fixed.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Ran `compile.py` on the Mac. It compiled 3 waiting logs (9/27, 9/29, 9/30) with no failures. Articles went 298 → 318. Cost $3.68.\n- Dropped the Mac's duplicate `42Analytics` index line and kept the Windows line instead, to avoid a conflict.\n- Left `~/.claude/settings.json` uncommitted on purpose, so a Mac-only hook path does not reach Windows.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- The compiler repo syncs through GitHub, but the daily logs and knowledge articles are per-machine. A run on one machine never covers the other.\n- Something on the Mac re-adds a second `dcg` guard hook with a Mac-only path. It came back within seconds of removal. Likely `dcg` re-installing itself.\n- `42Analytics` feeds Flint. Windows finished tier A waves 9–11.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- On Windows, in `Desktop\\DEV\\Flint`, run in order: `uv run scripts/triage_model_pass.py`, `uv run scripts/jev_pass.py state`, `uv run scripts/jev_pass.py run`. The run skips the 284 files Jev already did.\n- After the Jev run: Eric labels the answers, then Claude approves the shortlist.\n- Watch tonight's 21:00 `daily-backup` run. It is the first test of the two fixes (`global-memory` behind GitHub, `42Analytics` missing from the backup list).\n- If the duplicate `dcg` hook returns in `settings.json`, investigate the cause.\n\n## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash, Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds football GPS and force-plate pipelines. Wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo on 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-first player-coach near Iowa, $90K+ floor, mid-2027. Read `~/DEV/ContextVault` before career or personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python/R. Two machines: Windows by day, Mac by evening (split 9/24). Tool paths: machine.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB. No cloud servers or schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Google Drive only; keep DuckDB local to avoid sync locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n- Staff-facing text: Athletic Performance (not S&C), Athletic Medicine (not sports medicine), Applied Performance (not sport science).\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Challenge vague or rehearsed reasoning: \"sharpened, not soothed.\"\n- Decide standard technical details, state the choice, and move on.\n- Build in small steps. Do not break mid-season work.\n- Authors direction when leadership does not; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo group.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads (max 5; park one to add one)\n- **Status report**: biweekly to 12/18. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline\n- **SW/Tech Review (10/1)**: Erin asked; NEXT Teams 10/2, ET by 11/30. →`SoftwareTech_Review/docs/`\n- **Ship queue (9/25)**: q2wk to a named user: RI5 #002 wk 9/28, intern M1 10/9, AP Digest ch1 10/23. →`RunningTheRoom/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **PP Recruiting (9/30)**: 017 sent-ready; 018 SHIP. NEXT send 018, walkthrough. →`PP_Recruiting/docs/`\n- **RTP CLA (9/26)**: L1–2 built. NEXT Tim/Jacob export; Joe → L3. →`RTP_Constraints_Course/docs/`\n\n## Parked (NEXT lives in each STATUS.md; `where-was-i.py` lists them)\n- Prioritization slate LIVE 5/5 →Tier A prioritization-slate\n- Personal site: scroll-craft LOCAL, Artifact live →Tier A personal-site-build-routes\n- FB matchups: EPA = PFF via `scripts/pff_epa_profile.py` →`MatchupAnalysis_FB2026`\n- Flint: NEXT Eric: OneDrive keep/drop, 2 verify →`Flint`\n- 42Analytics: NEXT captions (429s) on Mac →`42Analytics`\n- VB GRID: fix merged 10/2 (#2); await Mike's reply →`Volleyball_GRID`\n- Sleep sched: NEXT night/early-kickoff →`sleep-scheduler`\n- TypeSafe: MAINTENANCE; PR #31 merged as record →`TypeSafe_Sandbox`\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Machine split (9/24): Win=day, Mac=eve; Mac sole writer GPS/Perch/VALD.\n- Hooks: patch both copies for shared events. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- daily-backup: manifest entries alone copy nothing; sweep nested ~/.claude repos →handoff 09-19.\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private I-983 repo; folder has a space, repo an underscore.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Win: Drive sync, junction and renv traps →machine.md. PFF: swap Parquet on Drive, never the DB; `PFF_DB`.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture Mac only. Dashboard →command-centre.\n- Post-merge: delete merged branches unasked (verify) →Tier A post-merge-cleanup.\n- Sessions: start `where-was-i.py`, prune with `session-sweep.py` →Tier A session-hygiene.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- None open. (OKR gate + OKRs_2026-27 CLOSED 10/2: Eric not developing further.)\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4310 chars, updated 2026-10-02 11:26)\n\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"c30dd791-2ba7-4d19-98b5-461f15f5a10e","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"cab8040e-b8bb-4ebb-a3e7-1df65f035770","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"10985618-bc72-4940-b7f7-d2aa6197da4a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Review the conversation context below and respond with a concise summary\nof important items that should be preserved in the daily log.\nDo NOT use any tools — just return plain text.\n\nFormat your response as a structured daily log entry with these sections:\n\n**Context:** [One line about what the user was working on]\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- [Important Q&A or discussions]\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- [Any decisions with rationale]\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- [Gotchas, patterns, or insights discovered]\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [Follow-ups or TODOs mentioned]\n\nSkip anything that is:\n- Routine tool calls or file reads\n- Content that's trivial or obvious\n- Trivial back-and-forth or clarification exchanges\n\nOnly include sections that have actual content. If nothing is worth saving,\nrespond with exactly: FLUSH_OK\n\n## Conversation Context\n\n**User:** <command-message>startup</command-message>\n<command-name>/startup</command-name>\n\n**User:** Base directory for this skill: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/startup\n\n# Startup\n\nThe front door for a work session on either machine. This skill owns no logic of its own — it runs `dev-sync`, whose Step 5 loads context back in.\n\n## What to do\n\nInvoke the `dev-sync` skill and run it end to end, Step 0 through Step 5.\n\nDo not reimplement any of it here. If the sync behaviour or the context report needs to change, change `dev-sync/SKILL.md`. One home for the procedure, and this file is an alias so the command matches how Eric actually thinks about it: \"sync\" is what the machine does, \"startup\" is what he is doing.\n\n## After the report\n\n`dev-sync` Step 5 ends with a short \"picking up\" report and a stop. Hold there and wait for Eric to confirm or redirect. Do not begin the next task on your own — `docs/STATUS.md` records where the last session stopped, which is not the same as what he wants to do this morning.\n\nIf he names a repo when he invokes this, pass it through so Step 5 reports on that one instead of guessing from the most recent commit.\n\n## Not this\n\n- Not the morning brief. Calendar, email and the shape of the day belong to the `morning` / `morning-briefing` work. This skill answers \"where was I\", not \"what's my day\".\n- Not a memory loader. Global memory arrives through the SessionStart hook before this runs; do not re-read `USER.md` or `MEMORY.md` by hand.\n\n**User:** Base directory for this skill: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/dev-sync\n\n# DEV sync\n\nEric works on a Mac and a Windows laptop. GitHub (`erash11`, all private) is the transport. This skill makes the current machine match GitHub without losing anything on either side. It is the first thing to run when sitting down at a machine.\n\n## Step 0: find DEV (never depend on cwd)\n\nEric may run this from anywhere. Detect the machine and go to its DEV root:\n\n- macOS (`uname` = Darwin): `~/DEV`\n- Windows (PowerShell, or `$OS` = Windows_NT): `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV`\n\nIf that folder does not exist, stop and say so. Do not guess another location. Print the root you chose on the first line of output.\n\n## Step 1: config and skills\n\nThese are separate repos and the other machine may have changed them:\n\n1. `~/.claude` (repo `dotclaude`): `git pull`. If `~/.claude/machine.md` is missing, copy `machine.macos.md` or `machine.windows.md` to `machine.md` for this OS.\n2. `~/.claude/skills` (repo `claude-skills`): `git pull`, then run `sync-to-codex.sh` (Mac) or `sync-to-codex.ps1` (Windows) so Codex sees the shared skills.\n3. `~/.claude/global-memory` (repo `claude-global-memory`): `git pull`. **This is the only thing that carries memory between the two machines.** USER.md, MEMORY.md and the handoffs live here. `daily-backup` pushes this repo nightly, but nothing pulled it at the start of a session until this step existed, so a same-day handoff (worked on the Mac after lunch, picked up on Windows that evening) arrived with stale memory. Pull it before anything else reads it. If the pull brings in commits, say how many and name the newest, so it is visible that memory moved.\n\nIf any of the three has local uncommitted changes, commit them first with `<machine> snapshot before sync`, then pull, then push.\n\n4. **Mirror project memory across the two slugs.** Tier A memory is keyed by a slug built from the absolute path, so every project has a Mac folder (`-Users-kristinarash-DEV-X`) and a Windows folder (`C--Users-eric-rash-Desktop-DEV-X`) under `~/.claude/projects/`. dotclaude carries both to both machines, but each machine auto-loads only its own. Before this step existed, 43 memories written on the Mac never loaded on Windows (found 2026-09-24). After the pulls above, run:\n\n   - Mac: `python3 ~/.claude/skills/dev-sync/mirror_memory.py`\n   - Windows: `python $HOME\\.claude\\skills\\dev-sync\\mirror_memory.py`\n\n   It copies files one side lacks, lets the newer version win when both have one (frontmatter `modified:`, else last commit), merges `MEMORY.md` indexes line by line, creates the twin folder when a project has memory on only one machine, and never deletes. The older side of any replaced file stays in dotclaude history. Put its summary line in the Step 4 report. If it wrote anything, commit `~/.claude` with `<machine> memory mirror` and push, so the other machine gets the result on its next sync.\n\n   Mirroring is a union: a memory deleted on one machine comes back from the other. To retire a memory for good, delete it from both slug folders in the same commit.\n\nMerge note for `global-memory`: `transcripts/.errors.log` and `MEMORY.md` are both append-heavy and both machines write them. On conflict, keep both sides' content rather than choosing one — these are logs, not settings, and a dropped line is a lost memory.\n\n## Step 2: inventory (always show before changing anything)\n\nWalk `DEV/*`, `DEV/Football/*`, and `DEV/SpecialRequests/*`. Skip `.claude`, `_tools`, `_DRIVE` (a link to the Google Drive primary storage drive, not a project; never init it, never walk into it), `token-dashboard` (someone else's remote), and any folder ending in `_OLD_merged*`. For each folder report one of:\n\n- **repo + erash11 remote**: uncommitted count, unpushed count\n- **repo, no remote**: needs `gh repo create`\n- **not a repo**: needs init + create (use the `new-project` skill's file set)\n\nThen `gh repo list erash11 --limit 300 --json name` and list repos with no local folder. Skip: `dotclaude` (that is `~/.claude`), `SecondBrain`, `claude-skills`, `claude-memory-compiler`, `DEV`, `DexaProject`.\n\nEric pre-approves these operations; the inventory is for visibility, not a pause for approval, unless something looks wrong (a repo with hundreds of uncommitted files, a large file about to be added).\n\n**Group the report by activity, not alphabetically.** Run `~/.claude/scripts/dev-activity.sh` (Mac) or `dev-activity.ps1` (Windows), which ranks repos by distinct commit days over 90 days. List the active ones individually and collapse the rest to a count: \"118 dormant repos: all clean\" is the useful line, not 118 names. About 14 repos are genuinely worked at any time; the rest are archives that happen to live in the same folder.\n\nRank by **commit days, not recency**. On 2026-09-09 a bulk migration gave 43 repos one commit each, after which every one of them looked active by any recency measure. Days-worked is not fooled by that, because a migration touches a repo once.\n\nNothing is archived or moved on the strength of this. Sorting is a reporting concern. Moving a dormant repo into an archive folder would break three things at once: `harvest-dev-docs` reads `<dir>/docs/*.md` without recursing, so archived repos would silently drop out of the memsearch index; KeystoneOS `projects.json`, `memory-sources.json`, `memory-index-paths.json` and `discovery-roots.json` hold DEV paths; and Tier A project memory is keyed by a slug derived from the absolute path, so moving a repo orphans its memory. Checked 2026-09-12. Do not revive the archive idea without fixing all three first.\n\n## Step 3: apply, per repo\n\n1. If uncommitted: put any untracked file over 95 MB and any `.env` into `.gitignore`, then `git add -A && git commit -m \"<machine> snapshot before sync\"`.\n2. `git fetch origin` then `git merge --no-edit origin/<branch>`.\n3. On conflict: keep this machine's version, save the remote version beside it as `<file>.from-mac` or `<file>.from-windows` (whichever the other machine is), stage both, commit. Never discard either side. `.DS_Store` conflicts: just delete the file.\n\n   Save the remote side by **moving the file, never by redirecting stdout**. A\n   redirect re-encodes the bytes; on Windows that silently mangles every\n   non-ASCII character (an em-dash becomes `ΓÇö`) and adds a UTF-8 BOM, so the\n   saved copy is corrupt and every later diff shows a phantom first-line change.\n\n   - PowerShell: `git checkout --theirs -- <f>; Move-Item <f> <f>.from-mac; git checkout --ours -- <f>`\n   - bash: `git checkout --theirs -- <f> && mv <f> <f>.from-mac && git checkout --ours -- <f>`\n\n   Never run `git add -A` while `.git/MERGE_HEAD` exists — it stages the\n   conflict markers themselves and commits a broken file. Stage only the named\n   paths you just resolved.\n4. `git push`.\n5. Missing locally: `gh repo clone erash11/<name> DEV/<name>`.\n6. No remote: `gh repo create erash11/<name> --private --source=. --push`.\n\nWindows note: line-ending noise (`LF will be replaced by CRLF`) is normal. If a merge refuses to start because of phantom modifications, run `git add --renormalize .` and commit before merging.\n\nStale-lock note: if a commit or push fails with `cannot lock ref 'HEAD'`, check\n`pgrep -fl git` (or `Get-Process git`) first. If no git is running and the\n`.git/*.lock` file is zero bytes and older than the session, it is stale from an\ninterrupted run. Move it aside — do not delete it — then retry.\n\n## Step 4: verify and report\n\nRe-walk every repo. Report: DEV root used, config and skills pulled (yes/no), the memory-mirror summary line, repos checked, pulled, cloned, created, and any folder still dirty or unpushed. List every `.from-*` file created so Eric can reconcile them. Finish with one line: clean or not.\n\n## Step 5: pick up where the other machine left off\n\nSyncing git state is only half the job. The other half is loading the context that came with it, and nothing else does this — the SessionStart hook injects global memory, but project state lives in the repo, and Tier A project memory only crosses machines through the Step 1 mirror. This step is the only place that repo context gets read back in.\n\nDo this for the repo Eric names, or the one he is in. If he has not named one, pick from the ACTIVE list in Step 2, not from all 132: take the active repo with the most recent commit and say which you chose. A dormant repo receiving one migration commit must never win this, which is exactly what picking by recency across everything would do.\n\n1. Read `<repo>/AGENTS.md` (or `CLAUDE.md` if there is no AGENTS.md).\n2. Read `<repo>/docs/STATUS.md`.\n3. Read the newest file in `<repo>/docs/handoffs/`, if the folder exists and its newest file is within about two weeks.\n4. If `docs/STATUS.md` does not exist in a repo that has real work in it, say so — that repo is invisible to the other machine and closeout should have created it.\n\nThen report, in six lines or fewer, before doing any work:\n\n```\nPicking up: <repo>\nLast session: <date>, <mac|windows>\nWhere things stand: <two lines from STATUS.md Current state>\nBlocked on: <open questions, or \"nothing\">\nNext: <top one or two from STATUS.md Next>\n```\n\nStop there and wait for Eric to confirm or redirect. Do not start the next task off the back of STATUS.md on your own — it records where work stopped, not what he wants to do this morning.\n\n## Do not\n\n- Do not `rm` anything. Move to a `_to_delete` or `_OLD_*` folder if something must get out of the way.\n- Do not rebase or force-push.\n- Do not push DexaProject or any repo whose CLAUDE.md says its data must stay local.\n\n**Assistant:** Syncing now. One small merge conflict in a log file. I am fixing it by keeping both sides.\n\n**Assistant:** Union merge: drop the three marker lines, keep both blocks.\n\n**Assistant:** Now the inventory of all DEV repos.\n\n**Assistant:** Inventory done. 15 repos need a pull, 1 has one changed file. `_ARCHIVE` and the `docs` folders are not projects, so I skip them.\n\n**Assistant:** Now missing repos, then a re-check.\n\n**Assistant:** Both \"missing\" ones are fine. `claude-global-memory` lives in `~/.claude`. `Linda_Projects` is the `Linda Projects` folder (space in the name).\n\n**Assistant:** All clean. Now picking which project to report on.\n\n**Assistant:** Flint is newest. Reading its state.\n\n**Assistant:** Sync is done, and everything is clean on the Mac.\n\n**Sync**\n- Root: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV`.\n- Config, skills and memory are pulled and pushed. Skills are synced to Codex.\n- Memory brought in 4 commits from Windows. The newest is \"handoff: perch-pull confirmed complete\".\n- Both machines had written to the same log file, `transcripts/2026-10-02.md`. I kept both versions.\n- Memory mirror: 15 projects paired, nothing copied.\n- 150 folders checked. 15 repos got new commits from GitHub. `CommandCentre` had one changed log file (`server.out`), so I saved it and pushed it.\n- Nothing was cloned or created, and no `.from-*` files were made.\n- GitHub shows two repos that are not in DEV. Both are fine:\n  - `claude-global-memory` is in `~/.claude`.\n  - `Linda_Projects` is the `Linda Projects` folder.\n\n**Picking up: Flint.** It has the most recent work among your active repos.\n- **Last session:** 2026-10-03, about 8:40 AM, on Windows. These commits came in from GitHub.\n- **Where things stand:** All 73 OneDrive folders are marked keep. \"The System\" is checked, and it is already in the queue as W62. \"Black Book\" is in the queue as X5, Enhanced Edition. 42Analytics is in the vault and its review is closed (151 entries).\n- **Blocked on:** The next Jev pass can only run on Windows, because the file catalog is only there. You also still owe three things: the Jev blind audit, the answers to the CLA primer questions, and listening to the CVASPS audio.\n- **Next:**\n  1. On Windows, run `uv run scripts/triage_model_pass.py`, then `uv run scripts/jev_pass.py state`, then `uv run scripts/jev_pass.py run`.\n  2. On either machine, keep ingesting books in queue order. The current queue comes first, then Wave 1, then Wave 2.\n\nWhat do you want to work on?\n\n**User:** lets pick up the Flint project\n\n**Assistant:** Next book step is *Supertraining* 4.3 and 4.4. The source PDF is not on this Mac yet. I am looking for it on Drive.\n\n**Assistant:** Found it. The Drive copy matches the checked file exactly (same md5). So I can do the next book step on this Mac.\n\n**Assistant:** Picking up the next book step: *Supertraining* sections 4.3 and 4.4 (14 pages). I pulled the vault first, because the Mac copy was out of date. Now I am starting a writer agent.\n\n**Assistant:** The writer agent is working now.\n\n**What I did**\n- The Mac does not have the Windows-only data that the Jev pass needs. So I picked the next queue item that can run here: *Supertraining* 4.3 and 4.4.\n- I copied the book PDF from Drive into `Flint/staging/books/`. It matches the checked copy exactly. Git ignores this folder.\n- I pulled the vault. It was missing the 42Analytics commits from Windows.\n- I started a writer agent. It turns the 14 pages into library entries.\n\n**What happens next**\n1. The writer finishes.\n2. A separate agent reviews the entries and tries to prove them wrong. Then I fix what it finds and run a second review.\n3. I stop and ask you before anything goes into the vault.\n\nYou do not need to do anything now."},"uuid":"4bba071d-5bad-4cf3-a952-dff217a64de4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:24:46.238Z","permissionMode":"auto","promptSource":"sdk","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"c30dd791-2ba7-4d19-98b5-461f15f5a10e","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
