{"parentUuid":"adaef7e5-6ea3-41a0-a1a0-4c4c9d891493","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"instructions","files":[{"path":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/CLAUDE.md","type":"User","content":"# Machine\n\nMachine-specific shell, paths, and traps live in `~/.claude/machine.md` (gitignored; copy `machine.macos.md` or `machine.windows.md` to `machine.md` on a new machine). It is imported below.\n\n@machine.md\n\nTwo machines share this setup: the MacBook (`~/DEV`) and the Baylor Windows laptop (`~/Desktop/DEV`). Projects sync through GitHub (`erash11`), documents through Google Drive.\n\n**Session routine.** When working inside a project repo, follow the Session routine in its `AGENTS.md`: `git pull` and read `docs/STATUS.md` at the start; update `STATUS.md`, commit, and `git push` at the end. This is what keeps the two machines in step.\n\n# Workflow Expectations\n\n- When asked to **create, merge, or consolidate an artifact** (calendar, deck, doc), finish with the produced file in hand. Check what exists first, then build the file even when a similar one already exists.\n- When the user types `/graphify`, invoke the Skill tool with `skill: \"graphify\"` before doing anything else.\n- Skills are tools: invoke one when it clearly fits the task.\n\n# Session Startup\n\nThe `keystone-unified-snapshot.js` SessionStart hook injects `global-memory/USER.md`, `global-memory/MEMORY.md`, a summary of `transcripts/.errors.log` (only when non-empty), and a pointer to the KeystoneOS snapshot. Work from the injected copy.\n\n- If hook failures appear, surface them once on the first response, then continue. Entries mean a hook or scheduled job failed.\n- If a **\"SessionStart hook could not read a required memory file\"** block appears, say so on the first response.\n- **If no `## User profile` block is in context at session start, the hook did not fire.** Say so, then read `USER.md`, `MEMORY.md` and `transcripts/.errors.log` by hand.\n- The injection is a frozen snapshot: mid-session writes take effect next session. Load anything further only when a question needs it.\n\n# Memory — routing only\n\nThe two-tier memory system (`~/.claude/global-memory/` and `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`) is owned by the **`memory-write` skill**, which holds the tier rules, character caps, slug rules, write steps, retrieval ladder, and daily-compiled-log rules.\n\nThe active project's Tier A memory (`~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`, indexed by its `MEMORY.md`) is **auto-injected by the harness** each session. Tier A files are also indexed by memsearch, so they stay recallable across projects.\n\n- Writing a fact — \"remember this\", \"note that\", \"save this\", \"forget about\" → `Skill(\"memory-write\")`\n- Recalling past context — \"did we already…?\", \"what did we decide about X\" → `Skill(\"memory-write\")`, retrieval-ladder section\n- Daily compiled logs at `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/daily/` are written only by `claude-memory-compiler`. Read one only when a question needs that day.\n- **Slugs differ per machine** (Mac `-Users-kristinarash-DEV-<X>`, Windows `C--Users-eric-rash-Desktop-DEV-<X>`). Tier A written on one is not auto-injected on the other; memsearch still finds it. Durable project facts belong in the repo's `docs/` so both machines see them.\n\n---\n\nMaintainer note: `AGENTS.md` diverges from this file on purpose (Codex has no Skill tool, so it spells out the memory rules). Keep them as separate files; the memory procedure's one home is the `memory-write` skill."},{"path":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/machine.md","type":"User","content":"# Shell / Environment (macOS)\n\n- zsh/bash is the native surface on this machine. PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`, `/opt/homebrew/bin/pwsh`) is installed only to run the KeystoneOS `.ps1` jobs natively.\n- **The tool-path list has one home: `~/.claude/TOOLPATHS.md`.** Read that file when you need a path. Only the traps below are kept, because acting on them wrongly is expensive.\n- **Verify, don't trust any list.** `which -a <tool>` is authoritative. To read PATH honestly, scrub the environment first:\n  `env -i HOME=\"$HOME\" USER=\"$USER\" TERM=xterm SHELL=/bin/zsh /bin/zsh -lic 'echo $PATH'`\n- Traps:\n  - Python is PEP 668-managed. Use venvs or `uv`, never `pip install` globally.\n  - `uv` is a standalone install at `~/.local/bin/uv`, **not** Homebrew. Claude Code hooks call `$HOME/.local/bin/uv`; do not delete or relocate it.\n  - `timeout` is NOT installed (no GNU coreutils). Don't use it in scripts.\n  - Homebrew owns `/opt/homebrew/bin`. `/usr/local/bin` is empty and stays that way.\n  - Secrets live in the macOS Keychain, not in `~/.zshrc`.\n- DEV projects live at `~/DEV` (not `~/Desktop/DEV`). Workspace map: `~/DEV/CLAUDE.md`.\n- Primary storage drive (documents, data, deliverables): `~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-eric.rash11@gmail.com/My Drive/00_Primary_Storage_Drive`."}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"566855af-e126-419e-bb21-6f044d218479","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:24:47.297Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCodebase and user instructions are shown below. Be sure to adhere to these instructions. IMPORTANT: These instructions OVERRIDE any default behavior and you MUST follow them exactly as written.\n\nContents of /Users/kristinarash/.claude/CLAUDE.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# Machine\n\nMachine-specific shell, paths, and traps live in `~/.claude/machine.md` (gitignored; copy `machine.macos.md` or `machine.windows.md` to `machine.md` on a new machine). It is imported below.\n\n@machine.md\n\nTwo machines share this setup: the MacBook (`~/DEV`) and the Baylor Windows laptop (`~/Desktop/DEV`). Projects sync through GitHub (`erash11`), documents through Google Drive.\n\n**Session routine.** When working inside a project repo, follow the Session routine in its `AGENTS.md`: `git pull` and read `docs/STATUS.md` at the start; update `STATUS.md`, commit, and `git push` at the end. This is what keeps the two machines in step.\n\n# Workflow Expectations\n\n- When asked to **create, merge, or consolidate an artifact** (calendar, deck, doc), finish with the produced file in hand. Check what exists first, then build the file even when a similar one already exists.\n- When the user types `/graphify`, invoke the Skill tool with `skill: \"graphify\"` before doing anything else.\n- Skills are tools: invoke one when it clearly fits the task.\n\n# Session Startup\n\nThe `keystone-unified-snapshot.js` SessionStart hook injects `global-memory/USER.md`, `global-memory/MEMORY.md`, a summary of `transcripts/.errors.log` (only when non-empty), and a pointer to the KeystoneOS snapshot. Work from the injected copy.\n\n- If hook failures appear, surface them once on the first response, then continue. Entries mean a hook or scheduled job failed.\n- If a **\"SessionStart hook could not read a required memory file\"** block appears, say so on the first response.\n- **If no `## User profile` block is in context at session start, the hook did not fire.** Say so, then read `USER.md`, `MEMORY.md` and `transcripts/.errors.log` by hand.\n- The injection is a frozen snapshot: mid-session writes take effect next session. Load anything further only when a question needs it.\n\n# Memory — routing only\n\nThe two-tier memory system (`~/.claude/global-memory/` and `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`) is owned by the **`memory-write` skill**, which holds the tier rules, character caps, slug rules, write steps, retrieval ladder, and daily-compiled-log rules.\n\nThe active project's Tier A memory (`~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`, indexed by its `MEMORY.md`) is **auto-injected by the harness** each session. Tier A files are also indexed by memsearch, so they stay recallable across projects.\n\n- Writing a fact — \"remember this\", \"note that\", \"save this\", \"forget about\" → `Skill(\"memory-write\")`\n- Recalling past context — \"did we already…?\", \"what did we decide about X\" → `Skill(\"memory-write\")`, retrieval-ladder section\n- Daily compiled logs at `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/daily/` are written only by `claude-memory-compiler`. Read one only when a question needs that day.\n- **Slugs differ per machine** (Mac `-Users-kristinarash-DEV-<X>`, Windows `C--Users-eric-rash-Desktop-DEV-<X>`). Tier A written on one is not auto-injected on the other; memsearch still finds it. Durable project facts belong in the repo's `docs/` so both machines see them.\n\n---\n\nMaintainer note: `AGENTS.md` diverges from this file on purpose (Codex has no Skill tool, so it spells out the memory rules). Keep them as separate files; the memory procedure's one home is the `memory-write` skill.\n\nContents of /Users/kristinarash/.claude/machine.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# Shell / Environment (macOS)\n\n- zsh/bash is the native surface on this machine. PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`, `/opt/homebrew/bin/pwsh`) is installed only to run the KeystoneOS `.ps1` jobs natively.\n- **The tool-path list has one home: `~/.claude/TOOLPATHS.md`.** Read that file when you need a path. Only the traps below are kept, because acting on them wrongly is expensive.\n- **Verify, don't trust any list.** `which -a <tool>` is authoritative. To read PATH honestly, scrub the environment first:\n  `env -i HOME=\"$HOME\" USER=\"$USER\" TERM=xterm SHELL=/bin/zsh /bin/zsh -lic 'echo $PATH'`\n- Traps:\n  - Python is PEP 668-managed. Use venvs or `uv`, never `pip install` globally.\n  - `uv` is a standalone install at `~/.local/bin/uv`, **not** Homebrew. Claude Code hooks call `$HOME/.local/bin/uv`; do not delete or relocate it.\n  - `timeout` is NOT installed (no GNU coreutils). Don't use it in scripts.\n  - Homebrew owns `/opt/homebrew/bin`. `/usr/local/bin` is empty and stays that way.\n  - Secrets live in the macOS Keychain, not in `~/.zshrc`.\n- DEV projects live at `~/DEV` (not `~/Desktop/DEV`). Workspace map: `~/DEV/CLAUDE.md`.\n- Primary storage drive (documents, data, deliverables): `~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-eric.rash11@gmail.com/My Drive/00_Primary_Storage_Drive`.\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"c30dd791-2ba7-4d19-98b5-461f15f5a10e","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"566855af-e126-419e-bb21-6f044d218479","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"session_context","context":{"userEmail":"The user's email address is eric.rash11@gmail.com. Use it only to identify the user, such as for authorship, attribution, or filtering their own work. Never send it to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL, or payload, unless the user explicitly asks."}},"type":"attachment","uuid":"db691400-6798-4c27-bec8-c47e7824b4e9","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:24:47.297Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nAs you answer the user's questions, you can use the following context:\n# userEmail\nThe user's email address is eric.rash11@gmail.com. Use it only to identify the user, such as for authorship, attribution, or filtering their own work. Never send it to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL, or payload, unless the user explicitly asks.\n\nIMPORTANT: this context may or may not be relevant to your tasks. You should not respond to this context unless it is highly relevant to your task.\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"c30dd791-2ba7-4d19-98b5-461f15f5a10e","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
